  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a conceptual sequence for how a major earthquake rupture zone may develop and then fail. It begins with a broad, damaged volume of rock surrounding a future fault, shown as a diffuse pink zone containing scattered small earthquakes and cracks, representing background seismicity. The next stage shows deformation becoming concentrated into a narrower shear zone: the activity and fractures align along a more distinct fault-like band, while large arrows indicate opposing motion on either side and emphasize shear localization.\n\nThe final part links this localized fault zone to loading through time. A three-dimensional sketch plots distance along the fault against time and suggests that stress is gradually built by slow slip and clustered foreshock sequences. Small clusters of foreshocks occur first, then a larger patch of slow slip expands and overlaps the active area. Near the end of this loading history, a small foreshock appears to trigger dynamic rupture at a starred point, implying that the main rupture starts only after progressive damage, localization, and stress accumulation combine. Overall, the image highlights a transition from distributed cracking to localized fault slip, culminating in earthquake nucleation.",
